// batch t: viena, palmares, jaomodo, narva, blenheim, poltava
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "hussardos alados": "Elite cavalry of Poland. They carried very long lances and, on their backs, frames of feathers that formed 'wings'. What the wings were for, historians debate: perhaps to frighten enemy horses, perhaps just to impress.",
  "mina": "A tunnel dug under a wall and filled with gunpowder. When it blew up, it opened a hole in the defenses. The defenders answered by digging countermines to find and disarm the tunnel.",
  "sapadores": "Soldiers specialized in digging trenches and tunnels close to enemy walls, under enemy fire.",
  "liga santa": "An alliance created in 1684 by Pope Innocent XI among the Holy Roman Empire, Poland and Venice to fight the Ottomans. Russia joined in 1686.",
  "batalhão": "An infantry unit of a few hundred men. In this period, European armies were counted in battalions (infantry) and squadrons (cavalry).",
  "esquadrão": "A small cavalry unit, from a few dozen to a few hundred horsemen.",
  "dragões": "Soldiers who traveled on horseback but, in general, fought on foot, as infantry. At Blenheim, some fought dismounted inside the villages.",
  "marechal": "The highest rank in the army in France and several other countries. A marshal commands an entire army.",
  "eleitor": "A German prince with the right to vote in the election of the Holy Roman Emperor. The Elector of Bavaria, Maximilian II Emanuel, was an ally of France.",
  "guerra da sucessão espanhola": "A war from 1701 to 1714 to decide who would inherit the throne of Spain, because King Charles II died childless. France wanted a grandson of Louis XIV on the throne. England, the Netherlands and Austria feared a united France and Spain.",
  "grande aliança": "The coalition of England, the Netherlands and Austria (plus several German princes) against the France of Louis XIV, in the War of the Spanish Succession.",
  "artilharia": "An army's cannon and the soldiers who serve them.",
  "regimento": "A unit of an army, made up of hundreds or thousands of men, with its own name, flag and history.",
  "quadrado": "A formation in which the infantry stands in a square or rectangle, with the soldiers facing outward, to resist cavalry attacks from any side.",
  "reduto": "A small enclosed fort, of earth and wood, that protects a point on the battlefield and forces the enemy to pass under its fire.",
  "fascinas": "Bundles of branches tied together, thrown into ditches so that soldiers can cross and climb up the other side.",
  "hetmã": "Chief of the Cossacks of Ukraine, in practice the ruler of the region. Mazepa was the hetman who switched sides and allied with the Swedes.",
  "cossacos": "A warrior people of horsemen from the frontier between Russia, Poland and the Ottoman Empire, who lived by war and governed themselves.",
  "canato": "Territory ruled by a khan.",
  "zungares": "A federation of Oirat tribes of western Mongolia. The Dzungar khanate was the last great nomad empire of the steppes, until the Qing destroyed it in 1757-58.",
  "khalkhas": "The Mongols of the east and center of present-day Mongolia, rivals of the Dzungars, who came from the west.",
  "qing": "A dynasty of Manchu origin that ruled China from 1644 to 1912.",
  "manchus": "A people of northeast Asia who conquered China in 1644 and founded the Qing dynasty.",
  "oito bandeiras": "The elite army of the Qing, divided into eight groups identified by colored banners, made up of Manchus, Mongols and Han Chinese.",
  "estandarte verde": "An army mostly of Han Chinese, made up of regional troops, which served alongside the Qing Eight Banners.",
  "quilombo": "A community of enslaved people who escaped and organized themselves on their own, far from their masters. Several villages, called mocambos, could form a quilombo.",
  "mocambo": "A village of escaped enslaved people, usually hidden in dense forest or mountains.",
  "bandeirantes": "Men from São Paulo who organized armed expeditions through the interior of Brazil to hunt Indigenous people, prospect for gold and, at times, destroy quilombos.",
  "paliçada": "A tall fence of pointed logs, used as a wooden wall."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  viena: {
    war: "Great Turkish War (1683–1699)",
    hook: "The afternoon when the greatest cavalry charge on record ended two months of siege.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Vienna was the capital of the Habsburgs, the family that ruled the Holy Roman Empire and Austria, and had long been in the Ottomans' sights. It guarded the Danube and the overland trade routes. In 1529, Sultan Suleiman had already tried to take it and failed, and after 1566 the Ottomans did not threaten the city again for more than a century.",
        "In 1683, the chance returned. The Ottoman Empire backed the Protestant rebels of Hungary, led by Imre Thököly, and mobilized its army in January 1682. War was declared in August of that year. Emperor Leopold I used the next 15 months to look for allies, and concluded with Poland the Treaty of Warsaw of 1683, which obliged the Poles to relieve Vienna if it was attacked.",
        { h: "The road to Vienna" },
        { timeline: [
          ["January 1682", "The Ottoman army is mobilized."],
          ["1683", "Leopold I and Poland sign the Treaty of Warsaw: mutual aid against the Ottomans."],
          ["14 July 1683", "The Ottoman army reaches Vienna. Kara Mustafa sends the customary demand for surrender, and it is refused. The emperor had already fled to Passau, and with him about 60,000 Viennese."],
          ["17 July", "Siege works begin: trenches, batteries and mines under the walls."],
          ["15 August", "Sobieski leaves Kraków with the Polish army."],
          ["6 September", "The Poles cross the Danube at Tulln and join the imperial and German troops."],
          ["12 September", "The battle on the slopes of the Kahlenberg."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Kara Mustafa, grand vizier" },
        "He was the [[grand vizier|vizir]] of Sultan Mehmed IV and commanded the army. According to one account, he disobeyed the sultan's orders, which said to take only frontier fortresses, and went straight to Vienna. He made little preparation for the army against an attack from outside, even with news of a large Christian force on its way. And he kept about 15,000 [[janissaries|janízaros]] and provincial soldiers in the trenches in front of the city, even when one of his commanders asked for the opposite. He was strangled in Belgrade on 25 December 1683, by order of the sultan.",
        { h: "Ernst Rüdiger von Starhemberg, garrison commander" },
        "He stayed in the city when the emperor left and commanded the defense throughout the siege. He had about 15,000 soldiers and 8,700 volunteers, and half of those who remained in the city, soldiers and civilians, died during the siege from all causes, including disease and hunger. He was later rewarded with 100,000 crowns, the Order of the Golden Fleece and the title of field marshal.",
        { h: "John III Sobieski, king of Poland" },
        "He already had decades of experience against the Ottomans, and for that reason command of the whole allied army went to him. Charles of Lorraine, commander of the imperial troops, took the left wing. Sobieski kept the Polish cavalry for himself and, at the end of the day, the charge.",
        { h: "Jerzy Kulczycki, the messenger" },
        "A Polish nobleman who spoke Turkish. The Viennese sent him across the Ottoman lines to tell the relief army the moment when the attack should take place."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers for the siege are debated. In the garrison, there are about 15,000 soldiers and 8,700 volunteers, with hundreds of cannon (one historian counts 312, of which only 141 were in usable condition). On the Ottoman side, the army that set out on campaign had some 90,000 men, but only part of it reached Vienna. A modern scholar, Şakul, speaks of about 65,000 soldiers and 25,000 support troops; another author reaches 150,000 present on 10 September, and older studies went as high as 300,000. Some argue that the Ottoman numbers were inflated.",
        "The relief army had, by general estimates, between 70,000 and 80,000 men, with 140 to 200 cannon. The split among Poles, Austrians and Germans changes from author to author: 18,000 to 27,000 Poles, some 18,000 to 20,000 Imperials, and 28,000 to 40,000 Germans (Saxons, Bavarians, Franconians, Swabians). Among the allies, there were Protestant Saxons beside Catholics; among the Ottomans, Christian troops from Wallachia and Moldavia.",
        { note: "A calculation cited on Wikipedia, without a weighty source, speaks of only 35,000 Ottomans on the battlefield, because the rest were in the trenches in front of the city or far from it. The battle card uses the highest estimate, 150,000, which belongs at the top of the ranges." },
        { h: "The mine war" },
        "For two months, Ottoman [[sappers|sapadores]] dug [[mines|mina]] under the walls. About 5,000 of them, according to one account. The explosions opened breaches of some 12 meters, and the defenders dug [[countermines|contramina]] to intercept the tunnels. On 8 September, the Ottomans took the Burg [[ravelin|ravelim]] and the low wall beside it. For the final blow, they prepared ten mines under the Löbel bastion, but the defenders found and disarmed them all.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "After crossing the Danube at Tulln, the allies came from the northwest, through the Vienna Woods, and had to come down the hills before reaching the city. The Kahlenberg, more than 480 meters high, dominates the landscape. The allies told the besieged of their arrival with bonfires lit on the mountain.",
        { h: "The battle in eight movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, at 4 in the morning of 12 September, the Ottomans attacked before the allies finished getting into position. The Ottoman right wing, under Kara Mehmed Pasha, fell on the allied artillery.",
        "Second, on the allied left wing, the imperial troops of Charles of Lorraine and the Saxons counterattacked and took the Nussberg. There was back-and-forth fighting at Kahlenbergerdorf and Nussdorf. Near noon, Lorraine stopped to reorganize the troops, and the Ottoman right was nearly destroyed.",
        "Third, in the center, Kara Mustafa pulled troops from other points to hold the flanks that were giving way, while some 15,000 men stayed in the trenches in front of the city. Around 2 p.m., Sobieski arrived at the Michaelerberg hill with the Poles. Near 4 p.m., they stopped at the Schafberg, waiting for the German infantry to do its part.",
        "Fourth, around 5 p.m., the troops of Franconia and Bavaria took the Türkenschanze, an Ottoman redoubt in the center, and the front of the Ottoman center collapsed.",
        "Fifth, there were smaller charges before the great one. One, of some 120 to 150 Polish hussars (the numbers vary), commanded by Sieniawski, broke two Turkish lines but lost a third of its men. Another, by Stanisław Potocki, was crushed, and he died. Neither decided the battle, but they showed that the Ottoman line could be attacked.",
        "Sixth, around 6 p.m., Sobieski sent four cavalry groups to attack at once, three Polish and one imperial. He himself went at the front, with the [[winged hussars|hussardos alados]] and other Polish horsemen, and his son Jakub rode with him. The charge broke the Ottoman line in the area of Baumgarten, Ottakring and Weinhaus.",
        "Seventh, the garrison of Vienna came out from behind the walls and joined the attack. Kara Mustafa tried to rally the troops and could not, and fled with the sacred standard. When the battle turned, he ordered the bombardment of the city stopped and commanded that the prisoners be killed.",
        "Eighth, the fighting ended around 10 p.m. Less than three hours after the charge, the allies had won."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Ottoman losses in the battle were about 15,000 dead, according to one Polish historian, or 8,000 to 15,000 dead and 5,000 to 10,000 prisoners, according to another. Over the whole siege, the Ottomans lost at least 20,000 men, counting disease and desertion. The relief army lost between 1,500 and 4,500, and about 1,300 of them were Poles.",
        "The plunder of the Ottoman camp was enormous, and the Poles got most of it, which angered the Germans and Austrians. Sobieski entered Vienna on the 13th, before the emperor, and that also offended the Austrians. On 28 September, he and Lorraine destroyed an Ottoman corps at Párkány.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In March 1684, Pope Innocent XI brought together the [[Holy League|liga santa]] with the Holy Roman Empire, Poland and Venice. Lorraine took Buda in 1686 and Belgrade in 1688. The Peace of Karlowitz, signed on 26 January 1699, handed a good part of Hungary to the Habsburgs. It was the end of Ottoman expansion in Europe and the start of the empire's slow retreat, and a shift in the balance of power in favor of the Habsburgs.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The charge: it is said to have been the largest cavalry charge in history, with about 18,000 horsemen. But the number changes depending on the source, and a charge like that did not happen in an instant: it was a sequence of attacks by several groups. Sobieski is supposed to have summed up the day with an adapted phrase of Caesar's: 'We came, we saw, God conquered'." },
        { note: "The coffee: legend says that sacks of beans left behind by the Turks were given to Kulczycki, who opened the first café in Vienna. Recent research says the first café was opened in 1685 by an Armenian merchant, Johannes Theodat (Diodato)." },
        { note: "Cross against crescent? Not quite. There were Protestant Saxons and Hungarians on different sides, and Christians from Wallachia and Moldavia in the Ottoman army. Some modern historians say the battle was more political than religious." },
        { note: "In 1684, a constellation, the Shield (Scutum), was named in reference to the battle. The original name was Sobieski's Shield." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  blenheim: {
    war: "War of the Spanish Succession (1701–1714)",
    hook: "The English general who deceived his own allies to march 400 km to the Danube and save Vienna.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1700, King Charles II of Spain died childless. Louis XIV of France wanted the Spanish throne for a grandson. England, the Netherlands and Austria feared a united France and Spain and formed the [[Grand Alliance|grande aliança]]. The [[War of the Spanish Succession|guerra da sucessão espanhola]] was beginning.",
        "France had a strong ally in the heart of Europe: Bavaria. In 1703, Marshal Villars and the Elector of Bavaria threatened Vienna. What saved the city was a quarrel between the two, and Villars was replaced by Marsin. But the danger remained: Austria was also fighting the Hungarian revolt of Rákóczi and another French army in northern Italy. In 1704, Vienna could fall.",
        "The English general John Churchill, Duke of Marlborough, then decided to take his army from the Netherlands to the Danube. For this he needed secrecy: according to one account, he deceived both the Dutch States General and the English Parliament about the march's destination.",
        { h: "The road to Blenheim" },
        { timeline: [
          ["19 May 1704", "Marlborough leaves Bedburg, near Cologne, with about 21,000 men, 16,000 of them British."],
          ["10 June", "He meets Prince Eugene of Savoy at Mundelsheim."],
          ["22 June", "He reaches the Danube and joins the imperial forces at Launsheim, after about 400 km in five weeks. He now had about 40,000 men."],
          ["2 July", "He takes the Schellenberg and, afterward, Donauwörth surrenders."],
          ["5 August", "Tallard, coming from the Rhine, joins the Elector of Bavaria and Marsin at Augsburg."],
          ["13 August", "The battle."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Marlborough and Eugene" },
        "Marlborough commanded the English, Dutch and several German princes' troops. Eugene of Savoy, born in France and in the service of the Habsburgs, commanded the imperial troops. The two met for the first time on 10 June 1704, and from then on fought side by side.",
        "The burning of Bavarian lands ordered by Marlborough was controversial.",
        { h: "Tallard, Marsin and the Elector of Bavaria" },
        "[[Marshal|marechal]] Tallard commanded the army that arrived from the Rhine and held the French right, beside the Danube. Ferdinand de Marsin and Maximilian II Emanuel, [[Elector|eleitor]] of Bavaria, commanded the left. The three held a meeting in the tower of the church at Blenheim, at 9 o'clock, to settle the plan. Tallard was convinced that the allies were retreating to Nördlingen. He was wrong."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The allies had about 52,000 men and 66 cannon. The French and Bavarians, about 56,000 and 90 cannon. The two sides were about the same size: it was maneuver, not numbers, that decided.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The battle front was almost 6 km long. The Danube protected the French right, and the hills of the Swabian Jura lay to their left. In front of the French line ran the Nebel stream, ringed by marshes. Blenheim (Blindheim in German, whence the name) stood near where the Nebel meets the Danube. Farther north came Oberglauheim and Lutzingen, with ditches and bushes that were hard on an attacker.",
        "The villages were full of infantry: in Blenheim, nine [[battalions|batalhão]] of Clérambault, with dismounted [[dragoons|dragões]] of d'Hautefeuille. In Oberglauheim, 14 battalions of Blainville, among them the Irish Brigade. In Lutzingen, five Bavarian battalions of Maffei and seven French ones of Rozel. Between the villages, Tallard's cavalry.",
        { h: "The battle in nine movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, in the early hours, the allies marched in eight columns and reached Schwenningen, 3 km from Blenheim, around 6 o'clock. The French [[artillery|artilharia]] opened fire at 8. Around 10, Cutts's column took two mills on the Nebel. Eugene was late, because of the terrain and enemy fire. A little after noon, Cadogan reported that he was ready, and Marlborough ordered the attack.",
        "Second, around 1 p.m., Cutts attacked Blenheim. Brigadier Rowe fell mortally wounded at the palisades, and the English attack stopped under French volleys. Eight [[squadrons|esquadrão]] of the Gens d'Armes, under Zurlauben, counterattacked, but Wilkes's Hessian brigade held and repelled them.",
        "Third, Clérambault, without consulting Tallard, put his reserve battalions inside the village. With so many men crammed in, the soldiers could barely shoot. Marlborough canceled a third attack and told Cutts only to contain the enemy. About 5,000 allies pinned down twice as many French.",
        "Fourth, on the allied right, Eugene attacked Lutzingen. The Prussian and Danish infantry of Leopold of Anhalt-Dessau crossed the Nebel and suffered heavily from the Bavarian battery. Eugene's cavalry had success at first, and was then repelled by Marsin's.",
        "Fifth, without cavalry, the infantry went back across the Nebel, and ten imperial standards were left with the Bavarians. A second attack, by the Duke of Württemberg-Teck, was also repelled, under crossfire from Lutzingen and Oberglauheim.",
        "Sixth, in the center, the brigades of Hulsen (Hessians and Hanoverians) and Orkney (British) crossed the Nebel, supported by dismounted dragoons and ten squadrons of cavalry, and covered the passage of Churchill's infantry and more cavalry.",
        "Seventh, around 5 p.m., the allies were ready. Marlborough's second line, under Bülow and Bothmer, broke the center and put Tallard's tired cavalry to flight. The nine French battalions remaining there were cut down by the cannon and by platoon fire at short range. Many fleeing horsemen drowned in the Danube.",
        "Eighth, Tallard was caught near Sonderheim and surrendered to a Hessian officer, who took him to Marlborough. Maximilian and Marsin withdrew in better order, but lost about 7,000 men to desertion on the way to Strasbourg.",
        "Ninth, in Blenheim, the French were trapped. Clérambault was not there, and the Marquis de Blanzac took command. Orkney proposed a parley, Brigadier de Nouville accepted, and around 9 p.m. Blanzac surrendered. About 10,000 French infantrymen laid down their arms."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The allies lost about 4,500 dead, 7,900 wounded and 500 prisoners. The French and Bavarians lost about 6,000 dead or drowned, 7,000 wounded and 14,000 prisoners. In all, French casualties passed 27,000.",
        "Bavaria fell into allied hands, and under the Treaty of Ilbersheim (7 November) came under Austrian military occupation. The Elector took refuge in the Spanish Netherlands. Tallard was taken to England and held at Nottingham until 1711. Before the end of the campaign, the allies also took Landau, Trier and Trarbach.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Blenheim saved Vienna and kept the Grand Alliance from falling apart. The war would last ten more years, until 1714. In February 1705, Queen Anne gave Marlborough the park of Woodstock and 240,000 pounds to build Blenheim Palace. The captured standards were paraded to Westminster Hall in January of that year.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The note: at the end of the day, Marlborough wrote to his wife, Sarah, on the back of a tavern bill, asking her to tell the queen that his army had won a glorious victory." },
        { note: "What changed in the war? Edward Creasy, in the 19th century, included Blenheim among the decisive battles of history. The historian John Lynn disagrees with that idea. The poet Robert Southey first criticized the battle in a poem, 'After Blenheim', and later praised it as a great British victory." },
        { note: "Winston Churchill, a descendant of Marlborough, wrote his biography. He described the army moving across Europe as 'a scarlet caterpillar'." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  poltava: {
    war: "Great Northern War (1700–1721)",
    hook: "On a July morning, Sweden lost its army and its rank as the great power of the north.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1700, Sweden was the master of the Baltic Sea. Russia, Denmark and Poland-Saxony attacked the kingdom together, thinking the king, an 18-year-old boy, would be easy to beat. Charles XII knocked Denmark out of the war within months and destroyed the Russian army at Narva. That was the start of the Great Northern War.",
        "In 1707, Charles invaded Russia, with the idea of marching on Moscow. Russia answered with scorched earth, winter and surprise attacks, and the Swedish army was pushed south, into Ukraine, where it hoped to join [[Ivan Mazepa|hetmã]], chief of the [[Cossacks|cossacos]], who had broken with Peter and changed sides.",
        "Nothing worked out. The reinforcements of General Lewenhaupt, about 12,000 men, were late, were attacked at Lesnaya and lost the supply convoy and about half the force. The Russian general Menshikov sacked Baturyn, Mazepa's headquarters, before Charles arrived. Food was short, and the winter of 1708-09, the coldest on record in Europe, weakened the Swedes even more.",
        { h: "The road to Poltava" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1707", "Charles XII invades Russia."],
          ["1708", "Defeat of Lewenhaupt at Lesnaya. Baturyn is sacked by the Russians."],
          ["Winter of 1708-09", "The coldest winter recorded in Europe."],
          ["2 May 1709", "The Swedes begin to besiege the town of Poltava, with about 20,000 men and 34 cannon."],
          ["May and June 1709", "A stray bullet hits Charles's foot, and command passes to Marshal Rehnskiöld. Peter arrives with about 80,000 men and sets up a fortified camp north of the town, by the Vorskla River."],
          ["8 July 1709", "The battle."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Charles XII, 27 years old" },
        "A soldier-king who, until then, had won almost everything. He was wounded in the foot shortly before the battle and, for that reason, could not command on his feet: he was carried on a litter. The litter would be destroyed during the battle, and he fled on horseback.",
        { h: "Peter the Great" },
        "His army had been destroyed at Narva, nine years earlier, and he had spent the time since rebuilding his troops. At Poltava, he himself commanded the 42 infantry battalions that came out of the camp. Menshikov and Bauer commanded the dragoon cavalry, on the left and the right.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Carl Gustaf Rehnskiöld, the Swedish marshal who commanded the attack. Lewenhaupt, who commanded ten battalions on the right. Carl Gustaf Creutz, of the cavalry. General Roos, whose column was cut off. Mazepa, who fled with Charles after the defeat."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Swedes assembled 16,500 men for the battle, and had about 20,000 in the months of the siege. They had only four cannon in the field: no artillery to open the Russian [[redoubts|reduto]]. Peter had 75,000 to 80,000 men in the region, but only some 42,000 fought, with 102 cannon.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Russian camp stood about 4 km north of Poltava. In front of it, running across the woods of Yakovetski and Budyschenski, the Russians had built ten T-shaped earthworks: six forts and four redoubts. Eight of them were garrisoned, with some 4,000 men, and behind them waited 10,000 of Menshikov's horsemen.",
        { h: "The battle in eight movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the Swedish plan was a night attack that would go around the redoubts. The infantry marched in four columns and got into position some 600 m from the redoubts, at 2:30 in the morning. But the cavalry, which was to accompany it in six columns, got lost, and a reconnaissance patrol raised the alarm. The surprise was gone.",
        "Second, around 4 o'clock, Rehnskiöld attacked anyway. Roos's column took the first two redoubts, but stopped in front of the third.",
        "Third, Lewenhaupt's wing went around the redoubts, and the Swedish cavalry scattered the Russian cavalry. But Rehnskiöld ordered the pursuit stopped and pulled Lewenhaupt back, to wait for Roos. And Roos was left isolated.",
        "Fourth, Peter took 42 battalions out of the camp, about 22,000 men, with artillery, and drew them up in line in front of the camp. Roos ended up surrendering, around 9:30.",
        "Fifth, around 9:45, the Swedish infantry attacked the Russian line and nearly broke it.",
        "Sixth, Creutz's cavalry could not reorganize in time to support the infantry. The Swedish left wing collapsed. The Russian infantry formed hollow [[squares|quadrado]], and Menshikov's cavalry attacked the Swedes from the rear.",
        "Seventh, the Swedish infantry fell apart. Charles, on the litter, made for the woods and then rode to the baggage train at Pushkarivka, where he arrived after 1 p.m. At night, he abandoned the siege.",
        "Eighth, Lewenhaupt tried to lead the rest of the army to the Dnieper, but the Russians caught up with him, and he surrendered at Perevolochna, on 11 July. Charles and Mazepa escaped with about 1,500 men to Bender, in Ottoman territory."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Swedish casualties were 9,700 to 12,200 killed, wounded and captured, plus about 2,800 prisoners counted separately. Among the distinguished prisoners were Rehnskiöld, Creutz and Piper, the king's minister. Russian casualties were about 4,600 to 5,950 killed and wounded. The official Russian figure is 1,345 dead and 3,290 wounded.",
        "After the battle, Peter gave a banquet in the field and toasted the captured Swedish generals, calling them 'masters of war'. Voltaire understood the gesture as a message to Peter's own generals. Many Swedish prisoners were sent to Siberia. Charles stayed five years in Bender and only returned to Sweden in December 1715.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Poltava was the largest and the decisive battle of the Great Northern War. It marked the end of Swedish dominance in the Baltic and the start of Russian supremacy in eastern Europe. The war only ended in 1721, with the Treaty of Nystad, and Russia came out of it as a great power.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The dates: Poltava was on 8 July in the Gregorian calendar, 27 June in the Julian calendar used in Russia, and 28 June in the Swedish calendar, which was one day ahead of the Julian. That is why dates in June and July 1709 change from book to book." },
        { note: "The battle site was declared a monument of national historical importance by Ukraine in 2009." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  narva: {
    war: "Great Northern War (1700–1721)",
    hook: "A snowstorm, an 18-year-old king and one November afternoon: 10,500 Swedes broke a Russian army more than three times larger.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "At the end of the 17th century, Russia was behind the armies of western Europe, and Peter the Great knew it. He also wanted an outlet to the Baltic and wanted to take back from Sweden the provinces that Russia had lost in the Time of Troubles. To do so, he joined Frederick IV of Denmark-Norway and Augustus the Strong, king of Poland-Lithuania and elector of Saxony. The three attacked Sweden at the same time.",
        "The Swedish king was 18. Charles XII landed at Humlebæk, north of Copenhagen, with the support of the English and Dutch navies, and Denmark left the war in August 1700, with the Peace of Travendal. That left Poland-Saxony, which was already in winter quarters, and Russia, which was besieging Narva, a Swedish town in Estonia. Charles decided to relieve Narva first.",
        { h: "The road to Narva" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1700", "Russia, Denmark and Poland-Saxony attack Sweden."],
          ["August 1700", "Denmark leaves the war, after Charles's landing near Copenhagen (Peace of Travendal)."],
          ["Late 1700", "Peter's troops besiege Narva."],
          ["30 November 1700", "The battle. In the Julian calendar, used in Russia, it was 19 November."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Charles XII, 18 years old" },
        "He went to Narva with a small army and preferred risk to waiting. Some Swedish officers wanted to postpone the attack because of the snowstorm. He ordered the advance. General Carl Gustaf Rehnskiöld commanded the north wing, and Otto Vellingk the south.",
        { h: "Peter the Great and the Duke of Croÿ" },
        "On the night before the battle, Peter left Narva and took Marshal Fyodor Golovin with him. Historians argue over whether it was cowardice or a calculation, and the reasons are not clear. He left in command Duke Charles Eugène de Croÿ, a foreigner, a diplomat without field experience, who is said to have accepted the post only the day before, after Peter talked him into it.",
        { h: "Other names" },
        "The three Russian divisions were those of Avtonom Golovin, Trubetskoy and Weide. The cavalry was under Sheremetev."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Swedes had about 10,500 men in the relief force (plus about 2,000 in the town's garrison) and 37 cannon. The Russians, according to the sources, between 33,000 and 40,000 men, with about 195 cannon. On the Russian side, the shortage of officers reached 70% at company level.",
        { h: "The Russian line" },
        "The Russians had shut off the town behind a line of trenches and barricades of about 6.4 km, between two rows of earthen walls. The line was too wide for the number of men, of uneven width, and the quarters hampered the movement of troops. In the center, the hill of Goldenhof was protected by obstacles of pointed stakes (chevaux-de-frise). The Swedes had the wind and snow at their backs, and the Russians in their faces.",
        { h: "The battle in eight movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, on the 30th, the Swedes lined up in two wings: Rehnskiöld, with 10 battalions, to the north, and Vellingk, with 11, to the south. About 4,300 horsemen covered the flanks.",
        "Second, around 2 p.m., the Swedes advanced. A snowstorm blew straight into the Russians' faces.",
        "Third, the Swedish columns filled the ditches with [[fascines|fascinas]] and crossed the Russian defenses in about 15 minutes.",
        "Fourth, the breaches opened and the Russian line split. The Russians, spread out in a thin line, had no way to gather force anywhere.",
        "Fifth, the Swedes rolled up the Russian line from both ends. Panic grew. Russian soldiers attacked the foreign officers, whom they did not trust.",
        "Sixth, the Duke of Croÿ surrendered. The crowd fleeing toward the Kamperholm bridge made the bridge collapse.",
        "Seventh, on the north wing, the Preobrazhensky and Semyonovsky regiments, Peter's guard, formed a [[square|quadrado]] behind a barricade of wagons and held out. Charles led attacks against them personally, and had a horse killed under him. Golovin and Trubetskoy ended up joining them. Weide's division, even with its general gravely wounded, counterattacked, but could not link up with the rest of the army.",
        "Eighth, the Russian command surrendered. The terms allowed the troops to leave with arms and colors, but the Swedes kept the generals. They lost almost all their artillery and wagons. Sheremetev took the cavalry south, to Vasknarva, and escaped."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Swedes lost about 667 dead and 1,247 wounded. The Russians, between 8,000 and 9,000 killed, wounded or drowned, and about 20,000 were captured, almost all released afterward (some 700 stayed prisoner). The sources give between 145 and 173 cannon lost and 171 standards. Some Russian regiments lost 57% to 68% of their men.",
        "Russia abandoned Ingria. Charles did not invade Russia: he turned south, against Augustus of Poland-Saxony. Vellingk wanted to attack Novgorod and Pskov. Peter answered by reforming the army: still in 1700, he formed 31 new [[regiments|regimento]].",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Narva showed what a small, well-trained and well-led army could do against a large, ill-prepared one. But the victory made Charles underestimate Russia. In 1704, Peter took Narva, and in 1709 he beat Charles at Poltava.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "They say that Peter, after the defeat, claimed that the Swedes would go on beating him for a while, but would end up teaching him how to beat them. The line is traditional and has no certain origin." },
        { note: "In 1900, Russia raised a monument to the dead of Narva. In 2000, Sweden unveiled a lion monument, with the inscription 'Svecia memor' ('Sweden remembers')." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  palmares: {
    war: "War against the Quilombo of Palmares (around 1680–1695)",
    hook: "The largest quilombo in the Americas resisted for almost a century, and its fall is still told in different ways.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Since 1602, the Portuguese colonists of Pernambuco had complained about enslaved people who escaped into the interior and formed [[mocambos|mocambo]]. In 1630, the Dutch took Recife, and the war between the Dutch and the Portuguese let thousands of enslaved people escape. Perhaps 90% of the Africans taken to Pernambuco came from Angola. Palmares also took in Indigenous people, caboclos and poor Portuguese, among them soldiers fleeing service.",
        "In the 1640s, several mocambos had joined together into larger kingdoms. The Dutch described Palmares Grande and Palmares Pequeno, each with a fortified central town of 5,000 to 6,000 people. Later, the whole was called Angola Janga, with nine large settlements. According to estimates, the population reached somewhere between 11,000 and 20,000 people in the 1690s.",
        { h: "The road to the fall" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1602", "Colonists already complain of fugitives forming mocambos."],
          ["1630", "The Dutch take Recife."],
          ["1676–77", "Fernão Carrilho attacks Palmares and wounds Ganga Zumba, who later asks for peace."],
          ["1678", "Ganga Zumba's agreement with the government, rejected by Zumbi."],
          ["1680–1686", "Six Portuguese expeditions fail."],
          ["February 1694", "The great stockade of Palmares falls."],
          ["20 November 1695", "Zumbi is killed."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Ganga Zumba and Zumbi" },
        "Ganga Zumba was the first king we can be sure of. After the attack of 1676-77, he approached the governor, who offered a pardon with conditions: the people of Palmares would go to live near the colonists and would return the enslaved people born outside Palmares. Zumbi rejected the terms. After the destruction of Cucaú, around 1680, he became the main leader of the resistance.",
        "Almost nothing is known with certainty about Zumbi's youth. The most repeated biography, with a birth in 1655, an upbringing by a priest and an escape at age 15, comes from Décio Freitas, in 1981, and rests on letters that have not been found.",
        { h: "Domingos Jorge Velho" },
        "A bandeirante from São Paulo, born around 1641 in Parnaíba, he had a reputation as a hunter of Indigenous peoples in the backlands. He was hired by the government of Pernambuco, together with Bernardo Vieira de Melo, to destroy Palmares."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On the attackers' side, the sources speak of about 6,000 men, well armed and with artillery: [[bandeirantes|bandeirantes]] from São Paulo, Indigenous allies and troops from Pernambuco. As for the defenders, there is no reliable number, and no document written by the people of Palmares survives. Everything we know comes from the point of view of the victors.",
        { h: "The place" },
        "Tradition says the target was the Cerca Real do Macaco, the capital, on a hill in the Serra da Barriga, in the present state of Alagoas. The documents of the time speak of the Cerca do Barriga. The link with Macaco is a reading from 20th-century historiography, repeated by authors such as Edison Carneiro and Décio Freitas.",
        { h: "The siege" },
        "The main source is the Rellação verdadeyra, written from the victors' side. It describes the fortification of the Barriga with ditches, caltrops (spikes set in the ground) and firearms. The first assaults caused casualties among the attackers. The Paulistas, according to one study, hauled two heavy cannon up the hill to breach the [[palisade|paliçada]]. The length of the siege varies by source, from 22 to 42 days.",
        "After hours of fighting between the troops outside and those inside the stockade, the Crown's forces got in. The date appears as January 1694, or 5, 6 or 7 February, depending on the author. The most common version is 6 February.",
        { note: "The precipice: the traditional version, from the earliest histories of Palmares, says the defenders were driven off a cliff, and that Zumbi threw himself over. The numbers (about 200 who fell) come from popular books and not from documents of the time. Later documents show that Zumbi lived until 1695." }
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "What happened afterward" },
        "Zumbi survived the fall of the stockade wounded and stayed a fugitive for almost 21 months. According to a letter from Governor Caetano de Melo e Castro, of 1696, a man close to him guided a troop of André Furtado de Mendonça to his hideout, and Zumbi died in combat on 20 November 1695. A version from 1757 says Antônio Soares killed him by stabbing. His head was displayed in a public place.",
        "Domingos Jorge Velho and his men received lands in the former territory of Angola Janga, to keep the kingdom from being rebuilt. Small mocambos remained in the region for decades.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Serra da Barriga became the Quilombo dos Palmares Memorial, opened in 2007. In the mid-1990s, Zumbi was recognized as a national hero. In 2011, a law established 20 November as Black Awareness Day, and in 2023 another law made the date a national holiday starting in 2024.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The population: estimates run from 11,000 to 20,000 people, and the main stockade, the Macaco, from about 6,000 to as many as 10,000 residents, depending on the author." },
        { note: "Capoeira: many people repeat that the people of Palmares fought with capoeira, but there are no documents that prove it." },
        { note: "Who were the people of Palmares? The historian Stuart Schwartz argues that Palmares was a mixed society, and the historian Alida Metcalf shows, through archaeology, the strong Indigenous influence. Historians also debate what slavery was like inside Palmares." },
        { note: "The name: Schwartz links the word quilombo to the Mbundu term ki-lombo, an initiation camp." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  jaomodo: {
    war: "Emperor Kangxi's campaigns against Galdan (1690–1697)",
    hook: "An emperor crossed the Gobi Desert with 235 cannon on the backs of camels, and it was the artillery that decided it.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the 17th century, Mongolia was divided. In the east and center lived the [[Khalkhas|khalkhas]]. In the west, the [[Dzungars|zungares]], who formed a strong [[khanate|canato]] under Galdan Boshugtu Khan. Across the border, the [[Qing|qing]] dynasty, of [[Manchu|manchus]] origin, ruled China.",
        "In 1687, troops of a Khalkha leader killed Galdan's brother. The next year, Galdan invaded the Khalkha lands and forced some 20,000 refugees, among them the spiritual leader Zanabazar, to flee south, into Inner Mongolia. The Khalkhas asked for the protection of the Qing.",
        { h: "The road to Jao Modo" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1687", "Troops of a Khalkha leader kill Galdan's brother."],
          ["1688", "Galdan invades the Khalkha lands."],
          ["September 1690", "At Ulan Butung, Khalkha and Qing troops ambush Galdan, who escapes."],
          ["1691", "The three Khalkha leaders declare themselves vassals of the Qing at Dolon Nor."],
          ["March 1696", "Kangxi leaves Beijing with about 80,000 men and 235 cannon."],
          ["12 June 1696", "The battle of Jao Modo."],
          ["1697", "Galdan dies near Khovd."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Emperor Kangxi" },
        "He was 42 and had already ruled for decades. In 1696, he decided to command in person the campaign that would finish Galdan. He crossed the Gobi at the head of one of the armies.",
        { h: "Galdan Boshugtu Khan" },
        "After being ambushed in 1690, he withdrew to the upper Kherlen River and stayed for about six years in that region.",
        { h: "Fiyanggu, Sun Sike and Queen Anu" },
        "Fiyanggu commanded the western army. Sun Sike commanded its center. Queen Anu, Galdan's wife, died in the battle."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Qing set out from Beijing with about 80,000 soldiers of the [[Eight Banners|oito bandeiras]] and the [[Green Standard|estandarte verde]] and with 235 cannon, carried by camels. They provided 1,333 wagons, each with six dan of grain. The army's numbers vary: one source speaks of 80,000 for Kangxi's army alone, another of 50,000 against 30,000 Dzungars, and others reach 100,000. The Dzungar numbers are even less certain: one account speaks of 5,000 men who ran into the western army, and another of 30,000.",
        "The idea was a three-pronged blow. Kangxi's army crossed the Gobi. Fiyanggu's, of 30,000 men (plus 10,000 in reinforcement), was to pin down Galdan. A third, of 10,000, stopped farther east and played no important part. The crossing took about 80 days.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The upper Terelj River, about 60 km east of Ulaanbaatar. The Dzungars had to fight in a small valley ringed by hills. Some sources place the site at Zuunmod, southwest of the capital, and the location is debated.",
        { h: "The battle in eight movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, Kangxi reached the Kherlen on 7 June and saw that Galdan had fled. Food was short, and he turned back. But Galdan, on the way, ran into Fiyanggu.",
        "Second, about 5,000 of Galdan's men ran head-on into the western army. Caught between the two Qing armies, they had to fight in the valley.",
        "Third, Sun Sike placed the soldiers of the Xi'an Banners and the Green Standard of Gansu and Shaanxi in the center. On the flanks stood the Manchu and Mongol horsemen.",
        "Fourth, the Qing drove off the Dzungar gunmen, took the surrounding hills and bombarded the Dzungars with cannon. They advanced behind a wooden barricade.",
        "Fifth, at noon, Galdan sent his men against the Qing center, which nearly gave way.",
        "Sixth, a detachment of Manchu cavalry attacked the Dzungar camp from behind and captured its provisions.",
        "Seventh, a Qing counterattack, supported by the artillery, broke the line. Galdan lost control of the troops, and many fled. Queen Anu died leading a charge that gave Galdan time to escape.",
        "Eighth, the encircled Dzungars were destroyed. Galdan fled with 40 or 50 guards."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Qing lost about 2,000 men. The Dzungars, about 8,000. Galdan fled to the Altai and died on 4 April 1697, near Khovd. The most cited version is illness, but some speak of poison. His nephew Tsewang Rabtan took over the khanate.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was considered the first time the Qing army successfully dominated rebel frontier tribes. Galdan's hope of uniting all of Mongolia ended, and a good part of today's Mongolia came under Qing control for about 200 years. The Dzungars would only be destroyed for good in 1758. The era of the great nomad empires was coming to an end: artillery and heavy firearms weighed more than the old cavalry of the steppes.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Anu's death: one source says the queen was killed by Qing artillery, and another that it was by arquebus fire, in the middle of a charge." },
        { note: "The modern name of the place, Zuunmod, means something like 'hundred trees' in Mongolian." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

